@startuml
'https://plantuml.com/class-diagram


interface IClient {
    +createResponse() : ResponseInterface
    +createResponseStream() : iterable
    .. for each implemented endpoint ..
    +endpoint() : ResponseInterface
    +endpointStream() : iterable
}
note left of IClient
    IClient defines a low-level interface to the AI provider's API.

    createResponse() and createResponseStream() call the create response
    endpoint on the AI provider:
    * createResponse() returns an actual response
    * createResponseStream() returns an iterable which outputs the stream as
      it's received: this can then be used to create a response

    It's expected other endpoint methods will follow the same pattern.

    (Another capability which could be added is the ability to background
    requests, as per https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/background.)
end note

IClient <|-- Client
class Client {
    -HttpClient $httpClient : used for making requests to AI provider
    -UriInterface $baseUrl : contains base URL and any query string parameters
    -array $authHeaders : contains authorization headers the AI provider requires
    __
    +createResponse() : ResponseInterface
    +createResponseStream() : iterable
    ..
    +endpoint() : ResponseInterface
    +endpointStream() : iterable
}
note left of Client
    The Client implementation is likely to be the only IClient implementation
    required, so long as only OpenAI-compatible AI providers are supported.

    It's possible that more implementations will be required, if Schoolbox
    supports providers which deviate subtly from OpenAI, or don't follow it at
    all.
end note

Client *-- Agent
class Agent {
    -IClient $client
    +request() : ResponseInterface
    +requestStream() : iterable
    -buildPayload() : array
}
note left of Agent
    An example implementation of an AI Agent.

    Note this class implements no interface: this is because each agent is
    likely to accept different parameters to its request() method, in order to
    build its payload before it gets sent to $client.

    Also: agents probably won't implement both request() and requestStream():
    they'll only implement a method which returns:
    * a full response
    * a stream
    * a reference to a job running in the background
end note

@enduml
